  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe 00z.KDTX RAOB sampled a very dry airmass last night, with PWAT \nof 0.61\" falling in the 10th percentile of daily sounding \nclimatology. The dry column is still in place this morning, with \nmostly clear conditions observed via satellite across Lower \nMichigan. Intervals of cloud anchored to a shortwave over the Upper \nMidwest will begin to work across Lower Michigan around mid-day, \nalthough not expecting any precipitation given how dry the resident \nairmass is. The wave combined with a shift to southwest flow signals \nthe pattern shift on tap for Sunday-Monday, with just one more day \nof seasonable temperatures in the mid-80s before warm and humid \nconditions arrive. \n\nMain drivers of this pattern shift are the amplifying ridge sprawled \nover western CONUS and a strong low over Hudson Bay. Placement of \nthese features puts the Great Lakes within the enhanced belt of NW \nflow aloft and in the path of multiple embedded shortwaves over the \nnext couple of days. Arc of instability advects into SE Michigan \nlate tonight-Sunday coincident with the arrival of left exit region\ndynamics, triggering shower/isolated thunderstorm chances as early \nas Sunday morning across the Saginaw Valley/Thumb. The instability \nplume then presumably expands into all of SE Michigan during the day \nSunday, with models signaling possible peak heating redevelopment \nfollowed by MCS potential overnight-Monday morning. The enhanced NW \nflow (35-45 knots at 500mb) will be supportive of organized \nconvection, but with low confidence in timing and coverage. SPC \nSWODY2 Marginal Risk reflects the uncertainty in both scenarios, \nwhere several models suggest a capping inversion is in place during\nthe afternoon and/or send the nocturnal MCS activity to our \nsouth/west toward the instability bullseye. For any thunderstorms \nthat do overcome these barriers, main concerns will be damaging wind \nand heavy rainfall. \n\nThe more organized severe weather potential still looks to be latter\nhalf of Monday with the arrival of the surface low and cold front. \nThis is going to depend largely on how convection unfolds throughout \nthe day, and how much instability is available to be engaged by the \nstronger forcing. Still looking at a lot of model variability for \nMonday, including thunderstorm coverage, timing of the frontal \npassage, etc. which makes it difficult to glean any meaningful \ndetails from the global/regional models beyond a broad severe \nweather threat. All modes of severe weather are possible including \nheavy rain, although damaging wind is again the primary threat. The \nlatest SWODY3 puts all of Lower Michigan in a Slight Risk of severe\nweather.\n\nThe other aspect of this airmass is the heat and humidity, in which \ntemperatures both Sunday and Monday climb toward 90 degrees. This \npushes heat indices into the low-mid 90s each afternoon, supported \nby a humid airmass with dewpoints in the 70s. More seasonable \ntemperatures then return mid-week behind the frontal passage, with \ndrier weather also anticipated.",
